{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *## Early Life and Succession* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father was assassinated. As he was still a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. \n\nRaymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly ten years. During his captivity *## Regency in Antioch* the Principality of Antioch was led by a regent as the prince Bohemond III was also a minor. Raymond was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid and he returned to Tripoli. \n\nUpon his return Raymond became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the Haute Cour and played a significant role in the succession crisis that followed the death of King Amalric I. *## Role in the Succession Crisis* Raymond supported the claim of Miles of Plancy to the throne but eventually backed down and supported the coronation of Baldwin IV. \n\nRaymond was a skilled military leader and played a key role in several battles against the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. He was a key advisor to Baldwin IV and helped to defend the kingdom against the growing threat of Saladin. Raymond died in 1187 after being wounded in the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his political acumen and was considered one of the most powerful nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.011065074242651463}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}